    "media:imagetext": "-2-\nyears ago went to sea with high hopes and for one reason or another,\nhave been forsaken by Lady Luck--or perhaps some other lady, or maybe\nJohn Barleycorn has won the decision.\nAnyway, instead of being Sir\nSomeone-or-Other in connand of the Queen Mary, they must be content\nwith four stripes on the well-worn, slightly frayed sleeve of an\n1l1-fitting shiney uniform of the Hong Kong and Macao Steam Naviga-\ntion Company, Ita. As we pulled out of Hong Kong, I noticed con-\nsiderable excitement on board and presently learned what it was all\nThe good ship Tai Ming (meaning Big Bright--probably named\nfor the same reason that a college boy six feet three inches tall is\ndubbed \"Shorty\") was pulling out for Wuchow and our routes were para-\n11el for the first ten miles.\nThe Captain and First Mate on the Tai\nMing were also Britishers of the same type as our officers. Well,\nthe Captain of our ship ran around like a boy yelling orders to the\nChinese engineer,\nthe pilot,\nWe steamed along side by side and\nI could visualize the race of the Robert E. Lee and the Natchez, only\nour ships shook to a propeller instead of side wheelers, and the roust-\nabouts were yellow instead of black.\nA junk got in our\nway and was\nmore casual than usual in getting\nout ol the way.\nour Captain, I\nsuspect, would nave run the junk to Davy Jones' locker rather than\nblow the whistle.\nThose Macao boats just simply can't maintain head-\nway and whistle at the same instant.\nThe junk captains believe their\ncralts are trailed by a long line of devils which formation is dis-\norganized whenever another ship, especially a big one, passes close\nastern, and it may take a long time for the devils to again pick up\nsurd\nthe trail.\nTherefore, the closer the miss, the better.\nWell, we got\ntur it will\n\"Yes lage\nall the devils! The Captain of our boat gave some\nof them back to\nExtrao\nthe junk skipper and made some remarks touching on the maternal ancestor\nof the junk captain which were not complimentary. By this time all\nwas excitement--we were clearly gaining on the Tai Ming and soon passed\nby her bow.\nThe Indian guards were interested, the Chinese crew in-\ndifferent, but the two old British officers were jubilant.\ndouru\nMollus\nWas Wid. Borro\n•We are now nearing Shanghai and I shall have to till out the\npolice form which 1s briet,\nbut I am always tempted to do like one\nfriend of ours did in Japan.\nThere you are presented with a police\nreport form to fill out every time you move around, anywhere.\nIriend Tilled out his form as follows: \"Name: Celery and Olives,\nSingle: Stewed corn,\nof What Country!\nFired of his menu, and\nthen he got in trouble.\nare not in Peiping on account of the skirmishes near there.\nmatter o ladde -\nas rags now",
